  "hints": [
    "The returned sequence is the only thing the caller sees, but the work is deciding quickly whether a value is already in it.",
    "Deletion does not need to preserve order: the rule lets you drop the last element into the hole and shrink the sequence by one slot.",
    "Keep a second structure mapping each stored value to the slot it currently sits in, and remember to move the entry of the value you shifted into the hole."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force keeps only the sequence itself. An insert asks `indexOf` whether the value is already there before appending, and a delete asks `indexOf` for the slot, overwrites that slot with the last element and pops the tail. Both questions are linear scans, so n instructions cost O(n\u00b2). The optimal solution hangs a hash map next to the sequence: `slot[value]` is the index that value currently occupies. The same lookup answers 'is it present?' and 'where is it?', so an insert is one map check plus an append and a delete is one map lookup plus one assignment, one pop and one map update. The sequence stays the only structure returned, so the answer is byte-for-byte the same array.",
    "why_optimal": "Every cell of `ops` has to be read once, which makes O(n) time the floor, and the sequence may end up holding almost every inserted value, so O(n) space is the floor for returning it. Both implementations use exactly those bounds. The improvement is structural rather than asymptotic in memory: the optimal replaces two linear scans per instruction with constant-time map operations, and the swap-with-last rule is what makes a constant-time delete possible at all, because it means the evicted slot can be filled without shifting the elements after it.",
    "pitfalls": "Three traps bite here. First, after the last element is moved into the freed slot its map entry must be rewritten; a stale index sends every later delete to the wrong slot. Second, when the deleted value is itself the last element, the value you write into the map and the value you delete from it are the same key, so the delete has to happen after the write, not before. Third, an insert of a value already present must not append a second copy, a delete of an absent value must touch nothing (not even a map entry), and a cell equal to 0 is neither an insert nor a delete."
  },
